  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B005",
    "B010"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks are a particularly mature quantum-physics achievement: the magic-wavelength approach permits exquisitely controlled interrogation of many atoms, and independently operating clocks have reproducibly reached the 10^-18 regime. This is more than an incremental improvement in timekeeping. It creates an experimentally usable regime for precision spectroscopy and sensitive tests of relativity. Katori's central lattice-clock concept and Ye's decisive experimental development provide a compact concept-to-realization attribution, provided the citation remains confined to lattice clocks.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains the essential reservation. Optical lattice clocks depend on a broad prior development of optical frequency metrology and on a substantial international community. We should not let a concise citation imply credit for that entire history, nor invoke future institutional decisions or applications as evidence already earned. Archival review must confirm that Katori and Ye alone adequately represent the prize-defining lattice-clock advance.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that independently reproduced 10^-18 performance and controlled systematics make lattice clocks prize-ready without waiting for a redefinition of the second.",
        "response": "I agree. Independent comparison is the demanding test here: it establishes that the remarkable performance is not a peculiarity of one apparatus or one analysis. I also agree that a redefinition of the second is neither necessary nor the proper basis for recognition. The fundamental achievement is controlled quantum measurement at this new level, while applications and institutional uptake are consequences rather than the main case."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson maintains that quantum error correction establishes the physical possibility of scalable quantum computation even though sustained fully fault-tolerant computation has not yet been achieved.",
        "response": "I accept the depth of the principle and its importance for present experiments. But the proposed three-person grouping spans codes, fault-tolerance constructions, and topological protection, so both the historical credit boundary and the experimental maturity of the full claim remain unsettled. B046 instead joins a sharply defined physical principle to repeatedly realized, independently tested performance; that makes it readier for a narrowly attributed award."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson emphasizes the direct and repeatedly reproduced observation of conductance quantization, conditional on resolving contemporaneous independent-discovery credit.",
        "response": "The directness of B028 is a serious strength, and its quantum transport phenomenon is unquestionably mature. Yet its unresolved parallel-discovery history is central rather than cosmetic: we cannot judge the proposed three names until priority and completeness are clear. For B046, the analogous attribution question is also important, but the supplied concept-to-realization account is presently more coherent and the citation can be tightly delimited."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival evidence supports credit limited to Katori and Ye for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks.",
      "How best to distinguish the lattice-clock achievement from enabling optical-clock and frequency-metrology work without understating essential contributors."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict with the proposed laureates is apparent. My long-standing collaboration with Anne L'Huillier concerns attosecond physics and does not involve the shortlisted preferred achievement."
  }
}
